    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d0bc8cd0_90c4c5f9",
        "filename": "wifi/1.0/default/wifi_legacy_hal.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 11
      },
      "lineNbr": 461,
      "author": {
        "id": 1081399
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-11-18T01:38:28Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Are we sure we want to present these as unsigned counts? I could imagine, for example, a vendor HAL using -1 to indicate error.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 461,
        "startChar": 13,
        "endLine": 461,
        "endChar": 21
      },
      "revId": "705baa8ddabb43bd6800794b5b7e994607eb8125",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d0bc8cd0_6bcad708",
        "filename": "wifi/1.0/default/wifi_legacy_hal.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 11
      },
      "lineNbr": 461,
      "author": {
        "id": 1074459
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-11-18T17:27:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t see anything in documentation of the header to indicate that they would want to do that. These are counters and we should probably keep them unsigned. Vendors should use the |wifi_error| to indicate any errors in stats retrieval.",
      "parentUuid": "d0bc8cd0_90c4c5f9",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 461,
        "startChar": 13,
        "endLine": 461,
        "endChar": 21
      },
      "revId": "705baa8ddabb43bd6800794b5b7e994607eb8125",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d0bc8cd0_b0562148",
        "filename": "wifi/1.0/default/wifi_legacy_hal.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 11
      },
      "lineNbr": 479,
      "author": {
        "id": 1081399
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-11-18T01:38:28Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "You might be able to avoid this, by using std::vector\u003c\u003e above (instead of std::array\u003c\u003e), and moving the vector out at the end.\n\n(The benefit of std::array is that we can allocate it on the stack, and avoid dynamic memory allocation. But, if we\u0027re returning std::vector, we\u0027re going to do a dynamic allocation in any case.)",
      "revId": "705baa8ddabb43bd6800794b5b7e994607eb8125",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
